`stashRestore` copies the whole dropped turn into the in-memory stash (`append([]byte(nil), taskBytes...)`, `internal/gateway/ctxrestore.go:125,132`). For an image turn that is the full base64 blob (hundreds of KB) resident in gateway RAM — up to `maxCtxRestoreEntriesPerSession = 8` per session across up to `maxCtxRestoreSessions = 8192` sessions (`ctxrestore.go:40,46`). A large-image session can pin real memory in the proxy.

**Options:**
- (a) spill media bytes to the durable CAS (see the durable-media-CAS issue) and keep only the digest + excerpt in RAM;
- (b) compress the stashed bytes;
- (c) a separate, smaller per-session media cap.

Text turns are naturally small; media is the outlier the current flat cap was not sized for.

## Parent context
Parent objective: prove advanced fak landing/storage paths have backups, guards, observability, and restart behavior. This is the cross-mechanism acceptance matrix, following #5990, #5997, #5998, and the existing wipref remote mirror/reconcile work.

## Problem
Durability features are currently proven package-by-package. There is no single crash/fault matrix that exercises the actual seams and prevents regressions across: checkpoint capture, local named refs, remote mirror/read-back, worker `commit-tree` candidate anchoring, trunk CAS, reclaim adoption, guarded cleanup, and operator status. A green narrow test can therefore miss a gap between mechanisms.

## Done condition
A hermetic test harness with real temporary Git repositories/bare remotes injects a stop after each durable boundary and restarts from a fresh process/repository where applicable. It proves:
1. crash before checkpoint ref update leaves source bytes untouched and no false checkpoint claim;
2. crash after local checkpoint ref preserves exact delta and `wip reconcile` action;
3. host loss after remote mirror recovers exact delta from a fresh clone and reports mirror freshness;
4. worker crash after `commit-tree` but before trunk CAS leaves a named local candidate (#5990);
5. host loss in that window recovers from remote worker-land mirror (#5997);
6. crash after trunk CAS classifies candidate LANDED without duplicate application;
7. concurrent/stale reclaim ownership follows #5998's CAS rules;
8. cleanup refuses every unlanded/local-only/unknown candidate and report mode mutates no refs;
9. remote outage never causes a false durable/replicated claim;
10. every row emits a typed JSON status and exact next action.

The harness records before/after refs, object reachability, working-tree/index bytes, remote refs, and process exit point. It runs on Windows and Unix CI without contacting a real origin.

## Scope
Integration harness, fault injection seam, fixtures, CI registration, and one machine-readable matrix artifact. No production behavior beyond seams needed to make existing guarantees testable.

## Work estimate
Estimate: 8 points. Uncertainty: operator-supplied estimate; revise through the parent denominator when evidence changes.

## Overall completion contribution
Contribution: 8/24 points.

